{% set local_problem %}
<p>Cullman's commitment to home maintenance means damaged fascia stands out — both visually and functionally. Peeling paint, water stains, and visible rot detract from otherwise well-maintained properties. More importantly, compromised fascia can't properly support gutters and allows water into roof structures where it causes invisible damage.</p>

<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Older homes near downtown</a> face particular challenges. Original fascia boards may be 50-80+ years old and approaching or exceeding their natural lifespan. Years of Alabama humidity and occasional gutter problems have taken their toll. These homes deserve repair work that respects their character while restoring full function.</p>

<p>Even newer homes can develop fascia problems if gutters have been overflowing or leaking. Water damage accumulates over time, and rot can spread behind paint that looks fine on the surface. By the time you notice, damage may have spread 3-4 times beyond the visible area.</p>

<p><strong>The uncomfortable truth:</strong> About 45% of gutter replacement jobs on pre-1960 Cullman homes need some fascia work. Not the entire house — usually isolated sections where gutters leaked or overflowed consistently. We'd rather tell you upfront than install new gutters on compromised wood that won't hold them.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We begin with thorough inspection — checking not just visible surfaces but probing for hidden damage behind paint and at seams. We press on fascia to check for soft spots; visual inspection alone misses hidden rot. You'll know exactly what condition your fascia is in before any work begins.</p>

<p><strong>For damaged sections:</strong> We carefully remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood back to solid material, and install new fascia boards. We use quality materials appropriate to your home — standard primed lumber for most applications, or matching profiles for historic homes where appearance matters.</p>

<p><strong>Material choice matters:</strong> Standard pine fascia lasts 10-12 years in Alabama humidity before showing moisture damage. Composite or treated lumber rated for ground contact lasts 20+ years. The upgrade costs 30-40% more but more than doubles the lifespan. For Cullman homes meant to last, it's worth considering.</p>

<p>New gutters are then installed on solid, properly prepared fascia — hangers at 24-inch intervals, proper slope for drainage, designed to prevent the overflow that caused damage in the first place. The result is a complete system: sound structure supporting quality gutters.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia in Cullman County for over 20 years. We understand that for this community, quality matters — both the structural quality that protects your home and the visual quality that maintains its character.</p>

<p>We take the time to do fascia work properly, using appropriate materials and careful craftsmanship. The repair should last as long as the home itself.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> If someone offers to install new gutters and says your fascia "looks fine" without actually getting up there and pressing on it, get a second opinion. We've replaced plenty of gutters installed 2-3 years ago on damaged fascia — the original installer either didn't notice or didn't mention it. Now the homeowner pays twice.</p>

<p><strong>Our advice:</strong> If you're replacing gutters and your existing ones have leaked for years, expect some fascia damage. Just the reality of Alabama humidity and older homes. Better to know about it and fix it now than discover it when your new gutters start sagging.</p>

